Wiring sketch:

const number = document.getElementById('cc-number');
const expiry = document.getElementById('cc-exp');
const cvc = document.getElementById('cc-csc');

number.addEventListener('input', () => {
  number.value = formatCardNumber(number.value);
  cvc.maxLength = detectBrand(number.value).cvc; // 4 for amex, 3 otherwise
});
expiry.addEventListener('input', () => { expiry.value = formatExpiry(expiry.value); });

number.addEventListener('blur', () => paint(number, validateNumber(number.value)));
expiry.addEventListener('blur', () => paint(expiry, validateExpiry(expiry.value)));
cvc.addEventListener('blur', () => paint(cvc, validateCvc(cvc.value, detectBrand(number.value).cvc)));

function paint(input, result) {
  input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!result.valid));
  document.getElementById(input.getAttribute('aria-describedby')).textContent = result.valid ? '' : result.message;
}

Notes:

- Validate on blur and on submit, not on every keystroke — Luhn flipping red mid-typing feels broken, and length errors are noisy while typing.
- These are UX checks, not security. Luhn passing means nothing about whether a card works; the processor authorizes. Never log card fields, and keep them out of analytics and session replay.
- Expiry equality matters: a 09/26 card on 2026-09-16 is still live (valid through month end) — that's the case most hand-rolled validators get wrong.
- Amex CVC is 4 digits; everything else in the table is 3. maxLength flips from detectBrand, no branchy logic needed.
- If you ship with hosted fields, most of this comes free from the processor and your server never sees the PAN — that's still the version I'd recommend for production.

