WHAT'S DELIBERATE IN THERE

- Token binding rides a hidden field, not the URL path — survives form re-renders and plays nicer with referrer-scrubbing proxies. Signed/opaque, resolved server-side to the employee key.
- action="/submit" with the server contract spelled out in comments: ledger write first, teach redirect second, discard third. The markup documents the boundary so whoever deploys it can't quietly violate it.
- type="text" + inputmode="email" instead of type="email": right mobile keyboard, no native validation gate that could silently swallow an odd submission. required stays — empty submits are noise, not signal.
- The honeypot does markup-level bot filtering; it pairs with the UA/IP fingerprinting at ingest. Anything that fills 'website' gets flagged, not counted.
- Zero external requests — no fonts, no CDNs, no images. Air-gap friendly, and no third party learns your simulation's visitor IPs.
- noindex + no-referrer in the head — the page should never be crawled, and the click's provenance shouldn't leak downstream in Referer headers.
- The beacon carries only the token and an event name — no PII in the wire payload; the server enriches from the token. Loss of that beacon is fine, which is why it's isolated from the submit path.
- The double-submit guard is edge idempotency, not scoring — it disables the button and relabels it, but never blocks the native POST.
- autocomplete="off" is the canonical default, but it's a fork worth knowing: many programs deliberately switch the password field to autocomplete="current-password" to test whether password managers save for a lookalike page. Whichever you pick has to match how you score PM-filled submits, or your metrics lie to you.
- stage=login is the chain hook — an MFA step is this same skeleton with stage=otp, a single six-digit input (inputmode="numeric"), and no autocomplete="one-time-code" unless the program wants to test SMS autofill behavior too.
- All color and radius values are CSS custom properties, so per-deployment theming is a variable swap — the neutral palette is a deliberate default, not a limitation.

One file, no dependencies, ready to be wrapped by whatever templating your platform uses — {{token}} and {{identifier}} are the only substitution points that matter.

THE CLIENT HANDLER — serialise, ship, guarantee the reveal

This supersedes the minimal script block in the template (file updated in place at /home/ubuntu/sim-templates/generic-signin.html). Standard handler, with the reliability layers that separate a toy from a platform. Here it is:

<script>
(function(){
  'use strict';

  var ENDPOINT     = '/submit';    // same-origin by design: no CORS preflight
  var VIEW_BEACON  = '/beacon';
  var TEACH_PATH   = '/taught/';
  var TIMEOUT_MS   = 1200;         // max wait on the wire before the reveal
  var PRIVACY_MODE = false;        // true => hash the secret client-side, never transmit it

  var form = document.getElementById('sim-form'),
      btn  = document.getElementById('sim-go'),
      tokEl= form.querySelector('input[name="t"]'),
      stgEl= form.querySelector('input[name="stage"]'),
      hpEl = form.querySelector('input[name="website"]'),
      idEl = form.querySelector('input[name="identifier"]'),
      secEl= form.querySelector('input[name="secret"]');

  var TOKEN = tokEl ? tokEl.value : '{{token}}',
      STAGE = stgEl ? stgEl.value : 'login',
      SID   = (self.crypto && crypto.randomUUID) ? crypto.randomUUID()
            : 's' + Date.now().toString(36) + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 10),
      T0    = Date.now(),
      SALT  = '{{salt}}';           // per-recipient salt; keep stable per user for reuse checks

  function sleep(ms){ return new Promise(function(r){ setTimeout(r, ms); }); }
  function goTeach(next){ location.assign(next || TEACH_PATH + encodeURIComponent(TOKEN)); }
  function toBlob(obj){ return new Blob([JSON.stringify(obj)], {type:'application/json'}); }

  // --- soft channel: view event. Loss acceptable; the submit is the score.
  try{
    if (navigator.sendBeacon) navigator.sendBeacon(VIEW_BEACON, toBlob({v:1, e:'view', t:TOKEN, sid:SID}));
  }catch(_){}

  // --- payload builder. Server derives IP/UA/geo from the request; we ship only what it can't.
  async function buildPayload(){
    var payload = {
      v: 1,
      e: 'submit',
      t: TOKEN,
      sid: SID,
      stage: STAGE,
      ts: Date.now(),                    // client clock, treated as untrusted server-side
      dwell_ms: Date.now() - T0,         // page init -> submit; deliberation signal
      hp: hpEl ? hpEl.value : '',        // ingest flags any non-empty value as a bot
      identifier: idEl ? idEl.value : '',
      secret_present: !!(secEl && secEl.value)
    };
    if (secEl && secEl.value){
      if (PRIVACY_MODE && crypto.subtle){
        var data = new TextEncoder().encode(SALT + ':' + secEl.value);
        var dig  = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', data);  // requires a secure context
        payload.secret_sha256 = Array.from(new Uint8Array(dig))
          .map(function(b){ return b.toString(16).padStart(2,'0'); }).join('');
      } else {
        payload.secret = secEl.value;    // standard mode: server discards after deriving the boolean
      }
    }
    return payload;
  }

  // --- primary transport: same-origin fetch, keepalive so navigation can't cancel it.
  function postJson(payload){
    return fetch(ENDPOINT, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
      keepalive: true,                   // request outlives the page (body stays < 64KB; ours is tiny)
      credentials: 'omit',
      cache: 'no-store'
    });
  }

  // --- belt-and-suspenders: if the fetch errors or dawdles, the browser's beacon queue
  //     still delivers it. At-least-once here + idempotent ingest server-side = effectively once.
  function fallbackBeacon(payload){
    try{ if (navigator.sendBeacon) navigator.sendBeacon(ENDPOINT, toBlob(payload)); }catch(_){}
  }

  // --- submit handler
  var sent = false;
  form.addEventListener('submit', async function(ev){
    ev.preventDefault();                 // JS owns submission now; no-JS falls back to the native POST
    if (sent) return;                    // double-click / Enter-repeat guard
    sent = true;
    btn.disabled = true;
    btn.textContent = 'Signing in\u2026';

    var payload, next = null;
    try{
      payload = await buildPayload();
      var res = await Promise.race([
        postJson(payload),
        sleep(TIMEOUT_MS).then(function(){ return null; })
      ]);
      if (res && res.ok){
        try{ var body = await res.json(); next = body && body.next ? body.next : null; }catch(_){}
      } else if (res === null){
        fallbackBeacon(payload);         // timed out; in-flight fetch may also land -> ingest dedupes
      }
    }catch(_){
      try{ fallbackBeacon(payload); }catch(__){}
    }finally{
      goTeach(next);                     // teach is unconditional; telemetry is best-effort
    }
  });
})();
</script>

WHAT'S DELIBERATE IN THERE

- preventDefault now, so JS owns submission — but the form keeps method/action, so a no-JS visitor still POSTs form-encoded and the server's other path catches them. Two ingestion shapes, one ledger.
- keepalive: true — the single most important flag in the block. Without it a navigation can cancel the in-flight POST; with it the request outlives the page. Payload stays far under the 64KB keepalive cap.
- The 1200ms race is a reveal-latency budget, not a network timeout. The human moment never waits on the wire; if the fetch hasn't answered by then, the beacon carries it and the page moves on.
- Timeout path can double-deliver (beacon plus the still-riding fetch) — that's fine by design. At-least-once transport plus the idempotent ingest we specced earlier equals effectively-once. Belt and suspenders beat a lost submit.
- goTeach sits in finally, unconditionally. Even if the handler throws, the reveal happens. A simulation that strands a clicker on a spinner has failed the exercise more badly than any missed telemetry.
- The payload ships only what the server can't derive: token, session ID, stage, dwell, honeypot, field values. IP, UA, geo, ASN come from the request itself at ingest. Less on the wire, less to trust.
- SID threads the chain: same UUID on the view beacon, the submit, and (server-side) the teach completion, so the pipeline can reconstruct view → submit → taught as one session.
- PRIVACY_MODE is the fork from the tracking briefing made concrete: flip it true and the secret is hashed client-side against a per-recipient salt — plaintext never leaves the browser. Needs a secure context (crypto.subtle) and a stable {{salt}} per user if you want password-reuse detection across campaigns.
- Same-origin endpoint: no CORS config, no preflight, no custom headers beyond Content-Type. The simplest deployment is the one with nothing to misconfigure.
- v:1 schema version in every event — cheap insurance for the day the payload shape evolves and old landing pages are still caches somewhere serving traffic.

Drop-in for any templating layer; the only substitutions are {{token}}, {{identifier}}, and {{salt}}. The form still works with JavaScript dead, the reveal still happens if the platform hiccups, and nothing but a hash ever crosses the wire if you tell it to. ⚡

